  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 152 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nOn Saturday, the above mentioned upper level trough axis slowly\nmoves from west to east across the region. While we continue to\nsee some differences in the operational GFS (stronger trough) and\nECMWF (weaker), we should see enough moisture and lift to yield a\ngood chance for rain, especially late Saturday through Sunday\nafternoon. With a decent chance for rain and increasing cloud\ncover, look for highs in the 60s to near 70 degrees on Saturday.\nThe high temperature forecast for Sunday will be tricky as most\nmodels indicate a front remaining to our north. However, given the\ntrends of the ECMWF in showing a stronger frontal push on Sunday,\nwe have opted to lower highs across portions of the northern Hill\nCountry into the I-35 corridor. If the cooler ECMWF verifies, we\nwill need to lower highs much farther south, perhaps as far south\nas I-10 in subsequent forecasts. Rain chances taper off Sunday\nnight as the upper level system slides east of the region. Given\nthe continued eastward movement of this upper system, rainfall\namounts will be lower along the Rio Grande, with near 1/4\"\nexpected, while farther east into the coastal plains and Highway\n77 corridor, up to 1.5\" will be possible. \n\nHighs on Monday will be much cooler, with lower 50s to lower 60s\nin the current forecast. With plenty of clouds and cold air\nadvection, suspect we will need to lower highs downward, with\nperhaps some upper 40s in the Hill Country. The cool temperatures\nwill linger into Tuesday with surface high pressure in place and\nplenty of cloud cover. Winds will begin to transition to a more\neast to southeasterly direction for Wednesday into Thursday as\nbroad southwest flow aloft sets up over the region. This in\naddition to the subtropical jet overhead will return some low\nprecipitation chances to the forecast for most areas.",
